# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Supabase cloud sync (optional — without it the sheet runs browser-only)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# OPTION A — Vercel project (recommended)
#   The Vercel ⇄ Supabase Marketplace integration already writes these into
#   the project and re-syncs them whenever Supabase rotates keys:
#     NEXT_PUBLIC_OPPORTUNTIES_SUPABASE_URL
#     NEXT_PUBLIC_OPPORTUNTIES_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY
#   The app reads them automatically (vite.config.ts → envPrefix). Nothing to
#   add by hand. For local dev just pull them:
#     vercel env pull .env.local
#
# OPTION B — manual (local dev without Vercel, or another host)
#   Supabase Dashboard → Project Settings → API Keys
#     Project URL       → VITE_SUPABASE_URL
#     Publishable key   → VITE_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY   (starts with sb_publishable_)
#   Copy this file to `.env.local`, fill in, restart `npm run dev`.
#
# Notes
#   • Do NOT use the legacy JWT "anon" key (starts with eyJ…). Supabase disabled
#     legacy keys on this project on 2026-09-03; the app will warn if it sees one.
#   • Never put sb_secret_… / service_role keys here — they are ignored and
#     would ship to the browser.
#   • Vite only exposes VITE_* and NEXT_PUBLIC_OPPORTUNTIES_SUPABASE_* names.
#   • Precedence: integration vars → VITE_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY →
#     VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY. A publishable key always beats a legacy key.

VITE_SUPABASE_URL=https://YOUR_PROJECT_REF.supabase.co
VITE_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=sb_publishable_...
